# Cross-platform disk manager

Uses `lsblk`, `fsck`, `smartctl`, `nvme`, and `udisksctl` on Linux;
`diskutil` and optional `smartctl` on macOS; and PowerShell Storage cmdlets on
Windows. It does not implement a filesystem, SMART parser, or recovery engine.

## Actions

### `show`

Lists physical disks, partitions, filesystems, labels, identifiers, mount
points, capacity, and read-only/removable state. Select `--json` for native
structured output.

### `health`

Shows OS reliability status and SMART data when `smartctl` is installed. Pass a
specific `--target` for full device attributes. SMART warnings are evidence to
back up and replace a disk, not something software can repair.

### `recover`

With no `--tool`, Linux and macOS attempt a read-only mount while Windows opens
TestDisk. `--tool testdisk` opens partition recovery. `--tool photorec` carves
files and requires an existing `--destination`. The script rejects a destination
nested under the source path, but cannot prove that it is on a different
physical disk; verify that yourself. On Windows it finds the official
`testdisk_win.exe` or `photorec_win.exe` and source selection stays inside that
tool; use an Administrator terminal. Stop writing to a damaged source first.

### `fix`

Runs a read-only verification first. Linux auto-repair is deliberately limited
to an unmounted ext2/3/4 filesystem: it uses `e2fsck -f -n`, then the
conservative preen mode `e2fsck -f -p`; reboot-required exit codes remain
successful but are called out in the command output. Other Linux filesystems
need their own repair guidance. macOS uses `diskutil verify/repair`. Windows
uses `Repair-Volume -Scan` then `-SpotFix` from an Administrator terminal. Back
up irreplaceable data before applying.

## Direct CLI

```sh
disk show
disk show --json
disk show 0
disk health --target /dev/nvme0
disk health C
disk recover /dev/sdb1 --tool mount
disk recover /dev/sdb --tool photorec --destination /mnt/recovery
disk recover --tool testdisk
disk fix /dev/sdb1
disk fix /dev/sdb1 --apply
```

Without `--apply`, recovery and repair print or run only read-only checks.
Direct applies require typing `APPLY`; the shared TUI confirms with `CLEAN` and
then supplies `--apply --yes`.
